        "titulo": "Numbers 21 to 100",
        "explicacao": "From 21 to 99, German says the ones first and then the tens, joined by «und»: 25 = fünfundzwanzig, \"five-and-twenty\" — like the old English \"four-and-twenty blackbirds\". «Eins» becomes «ein»: 21 = einundzwanzig. And it's all one word. Watch 16 and 60 (sechzehn, sechzig) and 17 and 70 (siebzehn, siebzig): they drop letters. 30 is dreißig, with ß. Hundreds simply add up: zweihundert (200), 101 = hunderteins. A thousand is «tausend». Phone numbers are usually read digit by digit.",
        "comparacao": "English says \"twenty-five\", tens first. German says \"five-and-twenty\", ones first. When you hear a number, wait for the end: in «sechsundachtzig», the 8 comes last, and it's the biggest part.",
